 "title": "Homotopy Type Theory: Univalent Foundations of Mathematics",
 "byline": "The Univalent Foundations Program, Institute for Advanced Study \u00b7 2013 (online build 1270-g3f17b85), CC BY-SA",
 "note": "The book is maintained on GitHub with nightly builds; content has been stable since 2013 apart from corrections. Exercises and chapter notes are not broken out. There is no official HTML edition. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "The collaborative \u201cHoTT book\u201d that set out univalent foundations: Martin-L\u00f6f dependent type theory read homotopically, where types are spaces (\u221e-groupoids), identity proofs are paths, and the univalence axiom makes equivalent types equal. Part I develops the foundations: basic type theory, the homotopical reading of identity types and function extensionality, sets and mere propositions with propositional truncation, the theory of equivalences, inductive types as initial algebras, higher inductive types (circles, spheres, pushouts, truncations, quotients) and homotopy n-types with connectedness and modalities. Part II applies this to mathematics: synthetic homotopy theory (\u03c0\u2081(S\u00b9), Freudenthal, Hopf fibration, van Kampen), univalent category theory and the Rezk completion, set theory with cardinals and ordinals, and constructive real numbers (Dedekind and Cauchy reals) plus surreal numbers. An appendix gives formal presentations of the theory."
